::ENG2_U04_L08_UA_Q01::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Source set: A student is researching whether pre-order lunch forms can reduce unwanted trays. Which research question is most focused for this inquiry?{
=A. How can student pre-order lunch forms help reduce unwanted trays?#Correct. This question is specific, researchable, and aligned to the unit inquiry.
~B. What is the history of every school lunch program?#Incorrect. This is too broad for the inquiry.
~C. Why do all students like different foods?#Incorrect. This is broad and not focused on unwanted trays.
~D. Are forms always good or always bad?#Incorrect. This is absolute and not research-focused.
}

::ENG2_U04_L08_UA_Q02::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Source: A district pilot report includes before-and-after tray data from a pre-order lunch program. Why is this source useful?{
~A. It has a formal title.#Incorrect. A title alone does not prove usefulness.
=B. It provides evidence directly related to the research question.#Correct. The source gives data about the exact topic being researched.
~C. It is long.#Incorrect. Length does not prove relevance or reliability.
~D. It mentions school in general.#Incorrect. General school information is not enough.
}

::ENG2_U04_L08_UA_Q03::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Original: "Reminders helped more students complete the pre-order form after the first week." Which paraphrase is accurate?{
~A. Reminders made the form unnecessary.#Incorrect. This changes the meaning.
~B. Students completed no forms after reminders.#Incorrect. This contradicts the source.
=C. After the first week, reminders increased student completion of the pre-order form.#Correct. This accurately restates the source in new wording.
~D. The form was removed after one week.#Incorrect. This is not stated.
}

::ENG2_U04_L08_UA_Q04::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which note best records source information without copying too much?{
~A. "The report says the thing was good."#Incorrect. This is vague.
~B. "All waste ended forever."#Incorrect. This overstates the source.
~C. "Lunch exists."#Incorrect. This is not useful research information.
=D. Pilot report: fewer unwanted trays after meals were prepared from student selections.#Correct. This note is concise and accurate.
}

::ENG2_U04_L08_UA_Q05::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which sentence best integrates source evidence?{
=A. A district pilot report found fewer unwanted trays after staff prepared meals from student pre-order selections.#Correct. This clearly identifies the source and finding.
~B. The report said stuff happened.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~C. Trays and forms were a thing.#Incorrect. This is informal and unclear.
~D. Everyone knows forms are good.#Incorrect. This is unsupported.
}

::ENG2_U04_L08_UA_Q06::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which sentence best explains why the evidence matters?{
~A. Evidence is always evidence.#Incorrect. This does not explain this evidence.
=B. The finding matters because student selections helped staff prepare meals students were more likely to choose.#Correct. This explains the connection between evidence and purpose.
~C. A source has information.#Incorrect. This is too general.
~D. Reports are written by people.#Incorrect. This does not explain relevance.
}

::ENG2_U04_L08_UA_Q07::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which revision best fits a student audience?{
~A. Pre-order compliance creates operational efficiency.#Incorrect. This is too formal for students.
~B. The thing helps lunch stuff.#Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
=C. Completing the form helps the cafeteria prepare meals students are more likely to choose.#Correct. This is clear and audience-aware.
~D. Food systems must optimize outputs.#Incorrect. This is unclear.
}

::ENG2_U04_L08_UA_Q08::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Which visual best supports the research finding?{
~A. A random hallway photo.#Incorrect. It does not connect to the evidence.
~B. A decorative background only.#Incorrect. Decoration alone does not explain evidence.
~C. A picture of unrelated school supplies.#Incorrect. This visual is unrelated.
=D. A labeled before-and-after chart showing unwanted trays before and after the pilot.#Correct. This visual directly supports the source finding.
}

::ENG2_U04_L08_UA_Q09::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which source would be least useful for the research question about pre-order forms and unwanted trays?{
=A. A blog post about unrelated sports uniforms.#Correct. This source does not address lunch forms or unwanted trays.
~B. A cafeteria manager interview about form completion.#Incorrect. This is relevant to the process.
~C. A pilot report with tray data.#Incorrect. This is directly relevant.
~D. A student feedback summary about understanding the form.#Incorrect. This is relevant to audience communication.
}

::ENG2_U04_L08_UA_Q10::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which transition best adds a related source finding?{
~A. However#Incorrect. This usually signals contrast.
=B. In addition#Correct. This transition adds related information.
~C. Instead#Incorrect. This signals replacement.
~D. Although#Incorrect. This introduces contrast or qualification.
}

::ENG2_U04_L08_UA_Q11::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which sentence is complete and clear?{
~A. Because the source shows fewer trays.#Incorrect. This is a fragment.
~B. Source trays fewer students because.#Incorrect. This is unclear.
=C. The source shows fewer unwanted trays after the pre-order pilot.#Correct. This is complete and clear.
~D. When the form and chart.#Incorrect. This is incomplete.
}

::ENG2_U04_L08_UA_Q12::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which presentation plan best shows collaboration?{
~A. One person speaks while the team ignores the source.#Incorrect. This does not show shared, source-based work.
~B. Everyone repeats the same unsupported opinion.#Incorrect. This is repetitive and not evidence-based.
~C. The team skips the visual explanation.#Incorrect. The audience needs the publication choices explained.
=D. One member explains the evidence, one explains the synthesis, one explains the visual, and one explains the revision choices.#Correct. This plan shows purposeful collaboration.
}

::ENG2_U04_L08_UA_Q13::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which synthesis best combines Source A and Source B? Source A: The pilot report found fewer unwanted trays. Source B: Reminders helped more students complete the form.{
=A. The pilot reduced unwanted trays, and reminders supported the process by helping more students complete the form.#Correct. This accurately combines both source ideas.
~B. The sources are both about school.#Incorrect. This is too general.
~C. Reminders proved the report was false.#Incorrect. This does not match the source information.
~D. The pilot had no connection to forms.#Incorrect. This contradicts the source set.
}

::ENG2_U04_L08_UA_Q14::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which sentence should be revised because it is not audience-aware?{
~A. Completing the form helps staff prepare meals students are more likely to choose.#Incorrect. This is student-friendly.
=B. Stakeholders must enact compliance with food-service optimization protocols.#Correct. This wording is too formal and unclear for students.
~C. The chart shows fewer unwanted trays after the pilot.#Incorrect. This is clear.
~D. A reminder can help students complete the form on time.#Incorrect. This is clear and relevant.
}

::ENG2_U04_L08_UA_Q15::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Which publication choice best matches Unit 4 standards?{
~A. A copied report with no audience revision.#Incorrect. The product should be adapted for its audience.
~B. A poster with no source evidence.#Incorrect. The product must be source-based.
=C. A student-facing flyer with accurate evidence, clear explanation, and a labeled visual.#Correct. This matches source-based writing and collaborative multimedia publication.
~D. A private note with no publication plan.#Incorrect. The unit includes publication.
}

::ENG2_U04_L08_UA_Q16::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which sentence overstates the evidence?{
~A. The pilot report found fewer unwanted trays.#Incorrect. This is measured and accurate.
~B. Reminders helped more students complete the form.#Incorrect. This accurately states the source finding.
~C. A chart can help students understand the change.#Incorrect. This is reasonable.
=D. Pre-order forms will eliminate all cafeteria waste forever.#Correct. This claims more than the evidence proves.
}

::ENG2_U04_L08_UA_Q17::[MLA.ENG2.R.10] Which paraphrase keeps the meaning of this source detail: "Students understood the purpose better when the message used simple wording and a labeled chart"?{
=A. Simple wording and a labeled chart helped students understand why the form mattered.#Correct. This restates the meaning accurately.
~B. Students did not need any message.#Incorrect. This contradicts the source detail.
~C. A chart made all problems disappear.#Incorrect. This overstates the source.
~D. Students only cared about colors.#Incorrect. This is not stated.
}

::ENG2_U04_L08_UA_Q18::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which sentence has the most appropriate academic voice?{
~A. The lunch form was super awesome.#Incorrect. This is informal and exaggerated.
=B. The pre-order process helped staff prepare meals based on student selections.#Correct. This is precise and evidence-based.
~C. The report said stuff.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~D. Students should do the thing.#Incorrect. This is vague and informal.
}

::ENG2_U04_L08_UA_Q19::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Which caption best supports a before-and-after chart?{
~A. This chart is colorful.#Incorrect. This does not explain the evidence.
~B. A chart appears here.#Incorrect. This is too vague.
=C. The chart shows fewer unwanted trays after the pre-order pilot.#Correct. This caption explains what the visual shows.
~D. Charts are useful sometimes.#Incorrect. This is general and unsupported.
}

::ENG2_U04_L08_UA_Q20::[MLA.ENG2.L.01] Which revision fixes the fragment: "Although some students needed reminders."{
~A. Although some students needed reminders and.#Incorrect. This is still incomplete.
~B. Some although students reminders.#Incorrect. This is unclear.
~C. Although reminders.#Incorrect. This is still a fragment.
=D. Although some students needed reminders, form completion improved after the first week.#Correct. This creates a complete sentence.
}

::ENG2_U04_L08_UA_Q21::[MLA.ENG2.RS.01] Which detail best supports the idea that communication affects form completion?{
=A. Student feedback said simple wording and a labeled chart helped students understand the form's purpose.#Correct. This source detail connects communication choices to student understanding.
~B. The cafeteria has tables.#Incorrect. This does not address communication or form completion.
~C. The school has hallways.#Incorrect. This is unrelated.
~D. Students take several classes.#Incorrect. This does not support the idea.
}

::ENG2_U04_L08_UA_Q22::[MLA.ENG2.W.04] Which question should guide final revision?{
~A. Can I make the wording harder so it sounds advanced?#Incorrect. Revision should improve clarity.
=B. Will the intended audience understand the evidence, explanation, visual, and action step?#Correct. This checks audience awareness and publication readiness.
~C. Can I remove the evidence to shorten the product?#Incorrect. The communication must remain source-based.
~D. Can I use a random visual to fill space?#Incorrect. Visuals should support meaning.
}

::ENG2_U04_L08_UA_Q23::[MLA.ENG2.C.01] Which oral explanation best supports the publication plan?{
~A. We picked the chart because it looked nice.#Incorrect. This does not explain how the visual supports evidence.
~B. We did not use the sources.#Incorrect. The publication must be source-based.
=C. We used a labeled chart because it shows the change in unwanted trays and helps students understand the evidence quickly.#Correct. This connects visual choice, evidence, and audience needs.
~D. We made the words complicated so adults would notice.#Incorrect. The audience is students.
}

::ENG2_U04_L08_UA_Q24::[MLA.ENG2.W.03] Which sentence best combines evidence, explanation, and audience purpose?{
~A. The report is about lunch and students should care.#Incorrect. This is vague.
~B. Lunch forms are a thing at school.#Incorrect. This is too general.
~C. The chart is nice and the form exists.#Incorrect. This does not explain the evidence.
=D. The pilot report found fewer unwanted trays after staff used student selections, so students should complete the form to help the cafeteria prepare meals they are more likely to choose.#Correct. This integrates evidence, explains why it matters, and gives an audience-focused action.
}

::ENG2_U04_L08_UA_Q25::[MLA.ENG2.C.03] Which final checklist best represents the Unit 4 workflow?{
=A. Research question, reliable source, accurate paraphrase, synthesis, expository explanation, audience revision, and purposeful visual.#Correct. This checklist follows the full research-to-publication process.
~B. Random topic, copied source, no explanation, unrelated visual.#Incorrect. This does not match Unit 4 standards.
~C. Opinion only, no source check, no revision.#Incorrect. Unit 4 requires source-based communication.
~D. Difficult words, hidden evidence, and decoration only.#Incorrect. Clarity, evidence, and audience fit are required.
}
